From: Realme X2 Pro Linux port <hmhmdcy@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] urs: stop mode services before switching to host role

When usb-signaller switches from device to host role, it deletes the
current configfs gadget. The matching OpenRC mode service (for example
usb-signaller-developer-mode) was left marked as started. On the next
host-to-device transition usb-signaller calls service_start() again, but
OpenRC treats it as a no-op, so the new usb0 netdev is never handed to
NetworkManager and unudhcpd keeps its stale socket.

Stop the known mode services before deleting the gadget. Returning to
device mode then starts them normally, letting the developer/tethering
scripts mark usb0 managed, activate the NCM connection and recreate the
DHCP server.

This is a generic usb-signaller lifecycle fix, not a board-specific
NetworkManager or configfs workaround.
---
diff --git a/src/urs.rs b/src/urs.rs
--- a/src/urs.rs
+++ b/src/urs.rs
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
  */
 
 use crate::gadget::{ConfigFsGadget, discover_gadgets};
+use crate::service::service_stop;
 use crate::sysfs::{sysfs_read, sysfs_write};
 use crate::udc::UDC;
 use crate::utils::dir_entries;
@@ -123,6 +124,11 @@
                         )?;
                         Self::wait_for_role(&switch_name, "host")?;
 
+                        println!("Stopping mode services before host role switch");
+                        let _ = service_stop("usb-signaller-developer-mode");
+                        let _ = service_stop("usb-signaller-tethering-mode");
+                        let _ = service_stop("usb-signaller-mtp-mode");
+
                         println!("Cleaning up gadgets after switch to host role");
                         for gadget_name in discover_gadgets() {
                             ConfigFsGadget::delete(gadget_name.as_str())?;
